L.CHAPTERS[10]={"name":"Ch 10 · The Seafloor and Continental Margins","short":"Ch 10"};
Object.assign(L.SOURCES,{"TB10":{"t":"Exploring Geology · supplied Chapter 10 PDFs","f":"Selected source-based learning content","tier":2,"unit":"section"},"C10A":{"t":"Chapter 10 Connect assignment · checked screenshots","f":"Selected source-based learning content","tier":2,"unit":"question"},"C10Q":{"t":"Chapter 10 Connect assessment · checked screenshots","f":"Selected source-based learning content","tier":2,"unit":"question"},"L15":{"t":"In-class Lecture 15 · ocean diagrams","f":"Selected source-based learning content","tier":2,"unit":"slide"},"T1015":{"t":"October 15 lecture transcript · ASR","f":"Selected source-based learning content","tier":2,"unit":""},"T1020":{"t":"October 20 lecture transcript · ASR","f":"Selected source-based learning content","tier":2,"unit":""}});
Object.assign(L.EXAM2_SOURCES,{"10.1":{"title":"10.1 Investigate the seafloor: observation versus inference","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.1; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Map the surface\nSonar uses sound travel time and a known sound speed to infer depth; a ship can map a swath rather than only the point directly below it.\nSubmersibles and remotely operated vehicles observe and sample restricted areas directly. A photograph supplies local detail; it does not map an entire basin.\nSatellite measurements of sea-surface height and gravity help infer broad seafloor relief. The satellite is not seeing through deep seawater to photograph the bottom.\nSample and image below it\nOcean drilling retrieves rock and sediment cores. Fossils, sediment properties, and basalt isotopes address different questions about environment, relative ages, and numerical ages.\nOxygen and carbon isotope measurements in appropriate marine records help investigate climate and ocean changes. An isotope ratio needs its geological context.\nSeismic reflection uses reflected waves to image buried layers and structures; it complements cores rather than directly collecting rock.\nAdditional sample evidence\nManganese-rich nodules are another seafloor resource discussed in the source. Their composition and occurrence provide a different investigation target from mapping depth or collecting a core.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.1-p2-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.1-p2-Im2.png","width":1640,"height":1216,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.1, page 2","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.1 · PDF page 2 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.2":{"title":"10.2 Ridges, new crust, and hydrothermal circulation","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.2; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Build oceanic crust\nAt a mid-ocean ridge, plates diverge; mantle rises and magma adds new oceanic crust. Submarine eruptions make pillow basalt, fissures solidify as dikes, and deeper magma crystallizes within the crust.\nNormal faults accommodate extension. Slow-spreading ridges commonly have a pronounced axial rift; fast-spreading ridges commonly have a broader, smoother crest.\nYoung hot crust is relatively buoyant. With distance and age it cools, becomes denser, and subsides; sediment can smooth its rough surface. Carbonate preservation also depends on water chemistry and dissolution depth.\nCirculate seawater and build vents\nCold seawater descends through fractures; heated water rises above a magma chamber, reacting with rock and carrying dissolved chemicals.\nWhen vent fluid mixes with cold seawater, sulfide particles precipitate. The dark plume and mineral chimney form through precipitation, not combustion.\nLecture 15 slide 9 connects black smokers with ore deposits and unique ecology. Chemical energy can support bacteria and the associated food web without sunlight.\nSource end members and preservation\nThe supplied fast-ridge example spreads at about 10 cm per year; the slow example spreads at about 1–2 cm per year and has a 1–3 km axial rift. These are textbook end members, not universal thresholds.\nBelow the local carbonate compensation depth (CCD), carbonate dissolution can match or exceed accumulation. The source gives about 4,500–5,000 m and explicitly says the actual depth varies geographically. Clay and silica-rich sediment can dominate below it.\nVent water commonly exceeds 350°C in the source description. Pressure in deep water prevents boiling under those conditions; hot water is still different from the magma that heats it.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.2-p5-Im6.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.2-p5-Im6.png","width":1451,"height":922,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.2, page 5","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.2 · PDF page 5 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.3":{"title":"10.3 Read ocean depth, sediment, and age maps","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.3; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Relief and sediment patterns\nContinental shelves are shallow; trenches are deepest. Ridges stand above surrounding abyssal seafloor but usually remain submerged.\nOld oceanic lithosphere cools and subsides. Abyssal plains can become smooth where sediment buries rough volcanic relief.\nSediment tends to be thicker on older crust and near major continental sediment sources. The Gulf offshore Texas and Louisiana combines large sediment supply with long accumulation.\nUse the age pattern\nThe youngest seafloor lies along spreading centers; age generally increases away from them. Use the color legend before interpreting a numbered map.\nThe Atlantic's paired age bands record spreading on both sides of the ridge. Pacific subduction has removed substantial older oceanic crust, so its surviving pattern differs.\nThe western Pacific is generally older and deeper than the region near the eastern spreading system. These broad relationships do not make every local depth depend only on age.\nGeographic map patterns\nThe supplied sediment maps emphasize thick accumulation offshore Texas and Louisiana. Ridges and isolated young crust generally have less sediment.\nBoth Atlantic basin sides can be deeper than the ridge crest; compare ridge elevation with older, cooled basin crust.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.3-p2-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.3-p2-Im2.png","width":750,"height":464,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.3, page 2","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.3 · PDF page 2 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.4":{"title":"10.4 Hotspots, island chains, and oceanic plateaus","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.4; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Read island and seamount history\nA volcanic edifice may grow above sea level, then become inactive as the plate carries it away from the hotspot. Cooling, subsidence, erosion, and wave planation can leave a submerged or flat-topped seamount.\nIn a simple fixed-hotspot model, the youngest active volcano marks one end of an age-progressive chain. Older islands record the plate's earlier locations.\nHawaii's young southeastern end and older northwestern islands indicate northwest plate motion relative to the hotspot. A chain's orientation alone is insufficient without ages or an active end.\nBuild a broad basalt plateau\nLecture 15 slide 12 shows rising mantle plume → submarine flood basalts → oceanic plateau. Its silhouette is broad and elevated above the adjacent deep seafloor.\nThe plume rises through the mantle as hot solid rock. Melting near the lithosphere supplies liquid magma; the plume is not a pipe of liquid magma extending through the whole mantle.\nRepeated widespread basalt eruptions build a plateau over millions of years. Kerguelen and Ontong Java are source examples. Hotspot settings occur in oceans and on continents.\nUse the supplied hotspot maps\nThe exercise includes hotspots west and south of Africa and on the African continent. Hotspots are not limited to ocean basins.\nAmong the American regions in the supplied exercise, western North America has the largest listed concentration. This is a result for that source comparison, not a numerical world inventory.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.4-p3-Im4.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.4-p3-Im4.png","width":1630,"height":472,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.4, page 3","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.4 · PDF page 3 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.5":{"title":"10.5 Island arcs, slab rollback, and backarc basins","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.5; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Generate arc magma\nAn ocean–ocean convergent boundary can form a trench and volcanic island arc. The descending slab lies beneath the overriding plate.\nWater released during metamorphic reactions in the slab enters the mantle wedge and helps generate melt. Water-assisted mantle melting is not wholesale melting of the descending plate.\nArc volcanic rocks commonly include andesite and composite volcanoes. Lesser Antilles, Aleutian, and Mariana systems are course examples.\nExplain the adjacent basin\nAs the subduction system retreats, slab rollback can move the trench and arc. Extension behind the arc can open a backarc basin.\nArc volcanism and backarc extension can overlap in time. Do not force the arc to cease before every backarc basin begins.\nThe curved geometry of an arc is related to a descending plate intersecting a curved Earth. A map-view curve by itself is not a plate-motion arrow.\nLocate the arc systems\nMany course arc examples flank Asia in the western Pacific and adjoining Indian Ocean. Read the boundary geometry as well as the geographic position.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.5-p2-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.5-p2-Im2.png","width":750,"height":574,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.5, page 2","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.5 · PDF page 2 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.6":{"title":"10.6 Pacific marginal seas have different origins","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.6; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Compare the tectonic histories\nThe Sea of Japan opened as the arc separated from Asia through backarc extension in the source reconstruction. The assessment's present-tense wording is not evidence that spreading continues today.\nParts of the Philippine Sea contain basins and separated arc remnants. A modern backarc basin lies behind the Mariana arc.\nThe western Bering Sea trapped oceanic crust when subduction shifted offshore. Its shallow eastern shelf belongs to the continental platform.\nSeparate flooded continents from new ocean crust\nShallow seas near China and between Australia and New Guinea occupy continental platforms. Being underwater does not make their crust oceanic.\nThe Gulf of California combines short spreading segments and transform faults after the plate boundary shifted inland. Baja moved north relative to mainland North America.\nBackarc opening, trapped ocean, flooded continental platform, and transform-linked rifting explain different seas; the word sea does not specify one origin.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.6-p1-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.6-p1-Im2.png","width":1000,"height":540,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.6, page 1","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.6 · PDF page 1 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.7":{"title":"10.7 Read the smaller seas around Eurasia","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.7; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Match a basin with its mechanism\nThe North Sea includes extensional basins where normal faults thinned continental crust; buried sedimentary systems include petroleum resources.\nThe Baltic occupies flooded, glacially and river-modified continental topography. Flooding is different from producing new oceanic crust.\nThe Mediterranean combines distinct histories: western rifting and eastern remnants of an older ocean modified by convergence.\nUse specific examples\nThe Black Sea includes a trapped remnant basin associated with the former Tethyan region and later flooding history.\nThe Persian Gulf lies beside the Zagros thrust belt; loading and flexure create a foreland basin.\nThe Red Sea records continental rifting and divergence. North Sea extension, Persian Gulf loading, and Red Sea separation should not be interchanged.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.7-p1-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.7-p1-Im2.png","width":1000,"height":508,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.7, page 1","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.7 · PDF page 1 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.8":{"title":"10.8 Reefs, carbonate platforms, and atolls","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.8; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Keep the environmental conditions together\nReef-building corals favor warm, clear, shallow water. Light supports photosynthetic partners; suspended sediment can block light or bury the reef.\nThe chapter and lecture emphasize tropical distributions, broadly near the equator. Clear shallow water near Iceland fails the warmth condition; tropical conditions alone fail if sediment obscures the light.\nA fringing reef borders the shore. A barrier reef is separated from land by a lagoon. A carbonate platform such as the Bahamas is broader than an individual volcanic-island reef.\nFollow the Darwin subsidence model\nA volcanic island initially supports a fringing reef. As the island cools, subsides, and erodes, coral can continue growing upward if suitable conditions persist.\nThe reef becomes separated from the shrinking island by a lagoon, then can remain as an atoll surrounding a lagoon after the island is submerged.\nReef growth must keep pace with relative submergence. The source also discusses alternative carbonate-platform histories; the sequence is a model, not the history of every carbonate rock.\nLocate reefs and test all conditions\nCaribbean, Bahamas, Philippines, and Polynesia are warm-water reef examples in the sources. Aleutian and Iceland examples fail the warmth condition.\nA clear shallow site still needs suitable temperature; a warm site still needs enough light and sufficiently low sediment interference.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.8-p4-Im7.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.8-p4-Im7.png","width":1919,"height":343,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.8, page 4","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.8 · PDF page 4 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.9":{"title":"10.9 Continental margins, canyons, and submarine fans","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.9; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Read the margin profile\nA typical profile progresses from the gently sloping shelf to steeper slope, then a sediment-rich rise and abyssal seafloor where those features are present.\nActive margins coincide with active plate boundaries; passive margins do not. Passive shelves are commonly wider, but shelf width alone is not the definition.\nRifting thins continental crust by normal faulting. A passive margin can preserve fault blocks buried beneath a thick sediment wedge, transitioning seaward to oceanic crust.\nTransport and deposit sediment\nLecture 15 slides 2–6 and October 20 develop turbidity currents, submarine canyons, and submarine fans. A dense mixture of water and sediment flows downslope under gravity.\nRepeated currents can incise canyons and carry sediment into deep water. Upper parts of some canyons also reflect river erosion during lower sea level.\nAs a flow spreads and slows near the slope's foot, it deposits a submarine fan. Coarse grains settling before fine grains leave a bed that fines upward; separate events can stack multiple turbidites.\nThe lecture's roughly 60 mph cable-break example illustrates an individual powerful event; it is not the speed of every turbidity current. Coherent submarine landslide blocks and mixed sediment-water currents are distinct end-member descriptions.\nRead the rocks as well as the relief\nThe passive-margin section changes from thinned continental crust of granitic composition to thinner oceanic crust composed of basalt and gabbro. Sedimentary cover lies above the crust and varies in thickness.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.9-p3-Im3.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.9-p3-Im3.png","width":1000,"height":306,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.9, page 3","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.9 · PDF page 3 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.10":{"title":"10.10 Evaporite basins and moving solid salt","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.10; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Accumulate evaporites\nEvaporation of restricted seawater precipitates salts such as halite, gypsum, and sylvite. A barrier can restrict exchange while repeated seawater supply sustains thick accumulation.\nWarm, dry conditions favor evaporation, but a thick deposit requires enough dissolved material and an appropriate basin history.\nHalite is NaCl. Mineral chemistry identifies the material; it does not by itself explain the basin geometry or prove a recalled exam formula.\nDeform after burial\nBuried salt is weak and comparatively low density. Under suitable loading it flows in the solid state and can rise into diapirs or domes.\nSalt can also provide a weak detachment, allowing overlying sediment to fold or slide. Salt glaciers represent another flow geometry.\nGulf Coast salt formed early in the basin's restricted marine history, was buried, and later moved upward and sideways. Salt structures can deform sediment and help create petroleum traps.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.10-p3-Im4.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.10-p3-Im4.png","width":1000,"height":572,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.10, page 3","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.10 · PDF page 3 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.11":{"title":"10.11 Reconstruct ocean opening and closing through time","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.11; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Follow the map sequence\nBefore breakup, Pangaea joined the major continents. Rift systems and new oceanic crust then separated parts of the supercontinent.\nIn the supplied reconstruction, the Central Atlantic opened before the South Atlantic, and the North Atlantic opened later. Ages and positions should be read from the actual maps.\nAround 150 Ma the central Atlantic and Gulf were developing; later Cretaceous configurations show wider southern Atlantic separation and continued northern reorganization.\nSeparate reconstruction from forecast\nThe Indian Ocean developed as fragments separated; later India–Asia convergence and Tethyan closure changed the arrangement.\nSpreading creates oceanic lithosphere; subduction consumes it. Their distribution explains Atlantic expansion and broad Pacific contraction in the course reconstruction.\nForward projections assume plate motions and boundaries persist. A future map is a conditional scenario, not an assured event; the October 15 garbled transcript does not override the checked map sequence.\nTimeline in the supplied reconstruction\nBefore about 200 Ma: Pangaea joins the major continental masses; subsequent rifting begins breakup.\nBy about 150 Ma: the Central Atlantic and Gulf are developing. Around 120 Ma: continued central and southern Atlantic opening and Gondwana breakup reorganize the Indian Ocean region.\nAt about 90 Ma: the South Atlantic separates South America and Africa; the northern Atlantic sector in the source has not yet opened fully.\nThe source places northern Atlantic opening near 80 Ma. By about 30 Ma, India–Asia collision and convergence near southern Europe nearly close the Tethys.\nThese dated maps are reconstruction snapshots. They support the central → south → north opening order without establishing the exact future arrangement.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.11-p2-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.11-p2-Im2.png","width":1021,"height":513,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.11, page 2","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.11 · PDF page 2 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.12":{"title":"10.12 Gulf and Caribbean: one region, several processes","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.12; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Observe the present boundaries\nThe Gulf includes broad shelves, deep central areas, and thick sediment. Large river input helps build the sediment cover along the northern margin.\nThe Lesser Antilles arc lies along the Caribbean's eastern side, where Atlantic lithosphere descends westward beneath the Caribbean in the supplied map interpretation.\nPacific subduction occurs along Central America; transforms occur elsewhere, including the Cayman region. Reefs and thick basaltic crust add further distinct features.\nConnect the history\nJurassic rifting and separation involving Yucatán opened the Gulf. Spreading later ceased there while sediment accumulation continued.\nThe course discusses a thick basaltic Caribbean plateau and a model involving entry from the Pacific, while noting that its origin is debated.\nRifting, sedimentation, plateau volcanism, subduction, and transform motion belong to different stages or locations. The text's later east-dipping phrase conflicts with its earlier map account; practice follows the mapped westward descent.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.12-p2-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.12-p2-Im2.png","width":1920,"height":1208,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.12, page 2","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.12 · PDF page 2 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.13":{"title":"10.13 Use the investigation map as evidence","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · 10.13; original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Infer processes from several observations\nThe investigation combines an old abyssal floor, a broad basalt plateau, an age-progressive island chain, an arc/trench system, and a narrow young spreading ocean.\nThe plateau is about 40 Ma. In the island chain, younger ages toward the active southeastern end support northwest plate motion relative to the hotspot.\nThe central margin's seismic section shows normal faults and thinned continental crust buried by Cenozoic sediment. Broad shelves, canyons, and fans add surface evidence of its passive-margin history.\nMake explicitly conditional predictions\nA narrow shelf on the western continent does not alone establish an active boundary. Check whether a trench, active boundary, or other diagnostic evidence is present.\nIn the narrow central/eastern ocean, youngest crust occurs at the middle ridge, with older crust toward its margins. In the constant-motion scenario, it was narrower in the past and widens in the future.\nAt 20 Ma before the depicted present, the plateau had traveled for about half its present 40-million-year interval and the island chain was shorter. Continued movement could carry the plateau toward the trench; collision is a possible model outcome.\nReconstruct the assigned scenario\nAt 20 Ma before the depicted present, the island chain was shorter and the plateau lay about half its present distance from its hotspot under the exercise’s steady-motion assumption.\nBoth the western narrow sea and the narrow central–eastern ocean were narrower in the source scenario. Continued divergence makes the central–eastern ocean nearly twice its depicted width after another 20 Ma.\nContinued motion may carry the plateau into the trench. The final checked cross-section choice was D; its original four alternatives are too small to grade reliably here. Use the readable textbook cross section to practice the matching extensional geometry.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.13-p1-Im2.png"}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.13-p1-Im2.png","width":1800,"height":811,"alt":"Supplied course figure 10.13, page 1","cap":"Exploring Geology · 10.13 · PDF page 1 · unchanged embedded source figure"}},"10.0":{"title":"10.0 The seafloor and continental margins","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 10 · original lab explanation","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Seafloor observations constrain oceanic crust, volcanic systems, sediment transport, continental margins, and basin history. Separate a directly measured observation from the geological history inferred from it.","image":"assets/figures/exam-chapter10/10.1-p2-Im2.png"}]}});
